Blackfin sim: commit a packed 16-bit ALU result through the store queue

decode_dsp32alu_0 writes one packed 16-bit ALU result straight into its
destination data register.  That is wrong when the DSP32 ALU operation sits in
slot 0 of a 64-bit multi-issue packet: the later slots must read the register
values from before the packet, and every register write commits only after all
three slots have executed.

CDJ-2000 GUI firmware relies on this:

    R0 = R0 -|- R0 || [P5] = R0 || NOP

With R0 = 7, real packet semantics store 7 through P5 and only then commit the
ALU result R0 = 0.  Committing early makes the parallel store write zero, and
the firmware falls into a fatal loop.

The fix uses the simulator's existing deferred STORE queue, matching every
other DSP32 ALU case in the same decoder.

Applies to: GDB 17.2, sim/bfin.

--- a/sim/bfin/bfin-sim.c
+++ b/sim/bfin/bfin-sim.c
@@ -4624,9 +4624,9 @@
       t0 &= 0xFFFF;
       t1 &= 0xFFFF;
       if (x)
-	SET_DREG (dst0, (t1 << 16) | t0);
+	STORE (DREG (dst0), (t1 << 16) | t0);
       else
-	SET_DREG (dst0, (t0 << 16) | t1);
+	STORE (DREG (dst0), (t0 << 16) | t1);
     }
   else if (aop == 1 && aopcde == 12 && x == 0 && s == 0 && HL == 0)
     {
